---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Foot Pods for Treadmill Running (2026): 5 Pace Sensors";
const description =
  "Five pace sensors still sold for treadmill runs and Zwift: Stryd, COROS POD 2, Garmin HRM 600 and the Runn belt sensor, plus Bluetooth vs ANT+ and calibration.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "stryd-5",
    name: "Stryd 5.0",
    category: "Best Overall Foot Pod for Treadmill",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Stryd+5.0+Footpod&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $299",
    priceBounds: {"low":299,"high":299},
    specs: "8 g shoe pod · ANT+ + Bluetooth · rechargeable, about 20 hr of running · on Zwift footpod list",
    pros: [
      "Broadcasts ANT+ and Bluetooth, so it fits almost any watch and app combination",
      "Stryd footpods are on Zwift’s compatible footpod list",
      "Stryd says it is accurate out of the box and that most users need no calibration factor",
      "Works on any treadmill and outdoors, so one sensor covers every run",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive for a pace sensor; the lower advertised price requires a paid membership commitment",
      "Sold mainly direct by Stryd in the US, and Apple Watch support runs through Stryd’s own app",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pod to buy if you want one sensor that works with Zwift and with most running watches. The price is the only real objection.",
    description: "Stryd is the one shoe pod that still covers nearly everything. The 5.0 is the current model: Stryd describes it as 15% smaller by volume than its predecessor, weighing 8 grams, with an extended antenna for more reliable pairing and a two-sided magnetic keychain charger. It connects over both ANT+ and Bluetooth, which Stryd states on its store and which independent reviews confirm. Stryd’s help centre lists compatible Garmin, COROS, Polar and Suunto watches and supports Apple Watch through the Stryd app, and Zwift names Stryd footpods among its compatible footpods. Stryd says the pod is accurate for pace and distance without calibration; owners on Zwift’s forum report mixed results, some seeing close agreement with a treadmill and others a small, consistent gap. Battery life is about 20 hours of running. On 15 October 2026 Stryd listed the pod at $299, or less with a commitment to its optional membership; a membership is not required to use the pod. It is primarily a running power meter, which some runners train by, but pace is all a treadmill runner needs from it.",
  },
  {
    id: "coros-pod-2",
    name: "COROS POD 2",
    category: "Best Foot Pod for COROS Watches",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=COROS+POD+2&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$99",
    priceBounds: {"low":99,"high":99},
    specs: "5.6 g pod · Bluetooth to COROS watches · 3 ATM · rechargeable with charging dock",
    pros: [
      "A third of the price of a Stryd",
      "COROS says it calibrates itself automatically and improves treadmill pace and distance",
      "Can be worn on the shoe or clipped to the waistband",
      "Adds running-form metrics such as ground time and stride height on COROS watches",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Works only with COROS watches; no Garmin, Apple Watch or Polar support",
      "Not on Zwift’s compatible footpod list, and cannot be paired alongside a Stryd on the same watch",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The obvious buy for a COROS owner who runs indoors. Anyone with another brand of watch should look elsewhere.",
    description: "If you already wear a COROS watch, this is the cheapest good answer on the page. COROS lists the POD 2 at $99 and in stock, and independent reviewers report the same price. COROS says the pod calibrates itself by measuring your stride and transmits treadmill pace and distance to the watch, and its help centre describes the POD 2 as the most reliable way to get indoor pace on COROS watches. It weighs 5.6 grams, is water resistant to 3 ATM and can be worn on the forefoot of the shoe or clipped centrally to the waistband. The limitation is absolute: COROS’s own FAQ says it works only with COROS watches, naming current models including the PACE 4 and APEX 4, but not the original PACE. It is not on Zwift’s footpod list. COROS also says a POD 2 and a Stryd cannot be connected to one watch together. Battery life is long, but listings disagree on the exact hours and standby days, so we have left the figure out.",
  },
  {
    id: "garmin-hrm-600",
    name: "Garmin HRM 600",
    category: "Best Treadmill Pace Sensor for Garmin Watches",
    badge: "Garmin Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F7Z6N7BR?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $170",
    priceBounds: {"low":170,"high":170},
    specs: "Chest strap · ANT+ + Bluetooth · rechargeable, up to 2 months at 1 hr/day · 5 ATM",
    pros: [
      "Garmin says it sends pace and distance from treadmill runs to compatible Garmin watches",
      "Nothing to clip on a shoe, and it is a full heart rate strap too",
      "Rechargeable, rated at up to two months at an hour a day",
      "Garmin’s own suggested replacement for its discontinued foot pod",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Pace and distance work only with compatible Garmin watches, and Garmin says not every watch supports every feature",
      "Not a speed source for Zwift or other brands’ watches, and a lot to pay if you only want pace",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For Garmin runners who want better treadmill pace and a heart rate strap in one. Check your watch model first.",
    description: "Garmin no longer sells a foot pod, and when a runner asked on Garmin’s forum where to find one, a Garmin staff member pointed to this strap. Garmin’s product page and launch announcement say the HRM 600 sends pace and distance data from indoor track and treadmill runs to compatible Garmin smartwatches, and independent retail listings repeat that it needs a compatible watch for those metrics. It is also a capable heart rate strap: it transmits over ANT+ and Bluetooth, records on its own and syncs later, is rated 5 ATM, and is rechargeable with up to two months of battery at an hour a day. Garmin listed it at $169.99 on 15 October 2026, matching the launch price. The catch is scope: this is not a general-purpose foot pod. It feeds Garmin watches, not Zwift as a speed source and not other brands. Garmin also notes that some watches do not support every advanced feature, so confirm treadmill pace for your exact model before buying.",
  },
  {
    id: "npe-runn",
    name: "NPE Runn Smart Treadmill Sensor",
    category: "Best Belt Sensor for a Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Measures the Belt",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=running+foot+pod&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $125",
    priceBounds: {"low":125,"high":125},
    specs: "Mounts on the treadmill · reads belt speed · ANT+ + Bluetooth · speed, incline and cadence",
    pros: [
      "Zwift names it as its compatible treadmill sensor",
      "Measures the belt, so it works for anyone who runs on that treadmill with nothing on their shoe",
      "Broadcasts speed, incline and cadence over ANT+ and Bluetooth",
      "Can show whether your console reads fast, because it does not depend on the console",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Installation involves adhesive mounting and marks on the belt, which reviewers say need renewing",
      "Tied to one treadmill; no use in a gym, a hotel or outdoors",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a household with one treadmill and more than one runner, or anyone who wants to check the console. Not for runners who train in more than one place.",
    description: "Runn takes the opposite approach to a foot pod. It fixes to the rear of the treadmill frame and times marks on the belt as they pass under its sensor, so it reports the belt’s actual speed whoever is running. North Pole Engineering says it works on virtually any treadmill, provides ANT+ and Bluetooth connectivity and tracks speed, incline and cadence; Zwift’s support page lists it as the treadmill sensor that adds Bluetooth speed transmission to compatible treadmills by reading belt speed. An independent review describes the details: a cradle stuck to the frame, the sensor set a fraction of an inch above the belt, and white stickers on the belt that wear and may need replacing. The same review reports that it ships factory-calibrated, that a Garmin watch pairs with it as an ANT+ foot pod, and that it charges over USB. NPE listed it at $124.99 and in stock on 15 October 2026. It is a home-treadmill device: it stays on one machine.",
  },
  {
    id: "stryd-duo-5",
    name: "Stryd Duo 5.0",
    category: "Two-Pod Option for Data-Led Runners",
    badge: "Premium Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=running+foot+pod&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $599",
    priceBounds: {"low":599,"high":599},
    specs: "Two 8 g shoe pods · ANT+ + Bluetooth · dual-sided metrics · magnetic keychain charging",
    pros: [
      "Everything the single Stryd 5.0 does, with a pod on each shoe",
      "Stryd says the second pod adds stability to power, pace and distance",
      "Left-right balance metrics for runners who track asymmetry",
      "Same ANT+ and Bluetooth compatibility and Zwift support as the single pod",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Twice the price of the single pod for extra metrics treadmill pace does not need",
      "Two batteries to keep charged instead of one",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Only for runners who already train with Stryd data and want left-right metrics. Everyone else should buy the single pod.",
    description: "The Duo is two Stryd 5.0 pods sold as a pair, one for each shoe. Stryd’s store says the second pod adds stability to power, pace and distance and enables dual-sided metrics such as left-right balance. Stryd listed the Duo 5.0 at $599 on its product page on 15 October 2026, which matches the regular price independent coverage reported at launch; Stryd also offers it at a lower upfront price with a membership commitment. A long-term independent reviewer of the Duo said its upgrades over the previous Stryd generation were not, on their own, a compelling reason to upgrade. For treadmill pace on its own, one pod is enough, and we would not pay twice as much for the second. It is on this page because some readers asking for the best Stryd for treadmill running will see it on Stryd’s store, and the honest answer is that the single 5.0 is the better buy for that job.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/guides/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Foot Pods for Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-foot-pods-for-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best foot pod for treadmill running?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most runners in October 2026, the Stryd 5.0, because it broadcasts both ANT+ and Bluetooth, pairs with most running watch brands and is on Zwift's compatible footpod list. COROS watch owners should buy the COROS POD 2 instead, which costs far less but works only with COROS watches. Garmin owners can use the HRM 600 chest strap for treadmill pace.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which foot pods work with Zwift?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Zwift's compatible hardware page lists the Zwift RunPod, Stryd footpods, the Polar Stride Sensor and the Garmin Foot Pod (ANT+ only), plus the Runn as a treadmill sensor. Of those, the RunPod and Garmin pod are discontinued and Polar no longer sells its sensor in the US, so Stryd and Runn are the practical new purchases. Zwift recommends calibrating a footpod before use.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Zwift RunPod still sold?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Zwift's own RunPod FAQ says the RunPod has been discontinued and is no longer available to purchase. It is still on Zwift's compatibility list, so a second-hand unit will pair, though at least one owner on Zwift's forum called it inaccurate. For a new purchase, a Stryd footpod or the Runn treadmill sensor are the options Zwift lists that are still sold.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the Garmin Running Dynamics Pod give pace on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not as a foot pod replacement. Garmin forum replies and independent comparisons describe it as reporting cadence and running-form metrics such as ground contact time and vertical oscillation, not pace or distance. It also appears to be discontinued: retailers list it that way and its US product page no longer loads. Garmin now points runners to the HRM 600 for treadmill pace.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to calibrate a foot pod on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on the pod. Stryd says its pod is accurate out of the box and most users need no calibration factor, and COROS says the POD 2 calibrates itself. Zwift still recommends calibrating any footpod before use. Whatever you do, check the treadmill's real belt speed first, because calibrating to a console that reads fast copies its error.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use a foot pod with an Apple Watch?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not through any method Apple documents for its own Workout app, as far as we could find. Stryd supports Apple Watch through the Stryd watch app, though its published compatibility list predates the newest models. The COROS POD 2 and Garmin HRM 600 do not work with Apple Watch for pace. On GymKit-equipped gym treadmills, the treadmill can send its data to the watch instead.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", url: "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], jobTitle: "Author and lab tester", worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-15",
      dateModified: "2026-10-15",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-foot-pods-for-treadmill/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-15",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-15",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best foot pod for treadmill",
      "treadmill foot pod",
      "foot pod for zwift",
      "stryd treadmill",
      "coros pod 2",
      "treadmill pace sensor",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#quick-picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestfootpodsfortreadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestfootpodsfortreadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Foot Pods <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">for Treadmill Running</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Most of the foot pods people still recommend for treadmills are gone: the Zwift RunPod, the Garmin Foot Pod and the Milestone Pod have all been discontinued. Here is what you can actually buy in October 2026, which watches and apps each one talks to, and how to tell whether you need a pod at all.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#compatibility" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Which Watch Works With Which Pod
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/" rel="author noopener" target="_blank" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Author and lab tester</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 15, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          sensors. On 15 October 2026 we checked each one against its maker's own store or product
          page (Stryd, COROS, Garmin and North Pole Engineering), the maker's help documentation
          where it exists, Zwift's "Compatible Running Hardware" support page, and at least one
          independent review or retailer listing. A figure appears in a card's specification line
          only where those sources agree; where they disagreed — battery hours on the COROS POD 2,
          for example — we left the figure out and say so in the text. None of these figures are
          our own measurements. Prices are those we saw on the makers' stores on 15 October 2026
          and change often, and compatibility lists change with firmware, so confirm the price and
          that the sensor pairs with your exact watch or app before you order. We earn a commission
          if you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we
          list or what we say about them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Overall:</span>
            <a href="#stryd-5" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Stryd 5.0</a>
            <a href="#stryd-5" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Best Value:</span>
            <a href="#coros-pod-2" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">COROS POD 2</a>
            <a href="#coros-pod-2" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Garmin Pick:</span>
            <a href="#garmin-hrm-600" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Garmin HRM 600</a>
            <a href="#garmin-hrm-600" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Measures the Belt:</span>
            <a href="#npe-runn" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">NPE Runn Smart Treadmill Sensor</a>
            <a href="#npe-runn" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Premium Pick:</span>
            <a href="#stryd-duo-5" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Stryd Duo 5.0</a>
            <a href="#stryd-duo-5" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 5 Best Foot Pods and Pace Sensors for Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose These Sensors</a>
          <a href="#bluetooth-vs-ant" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Bluetooth vs ANT+: Which Connection You Need</a>
          <a href="#compatibility" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Which Watches and Apps Each Sensor Works With</a>
          <a href="#calibration" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Calibration: What Each Sensor Asks of You</a>
          <a href="#battery" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Batteries, Charging and Living With a Sensor</a>
          <a href="#reading-listings" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. How to Read a Foot Pod Listing</a>
          <a href="#pod-or-alternative" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Foot Pod, Belt Sensor or a Connected Treadmill?</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A foot pod is the cheapest way to get honest treadmill pace into a watch or into Zwift,
